● I've built the demonstrator: go run ./src prints the three example trees, and
  go test ./... passes. I wrote spec.md first and derived code and tests from
  it. Both steps are committed to a new local git repo.

  Your decisions:
  - display() walks the tree depth-first (Pre-Order) instead of breadth-first
    ("Breite vor Tiefe") as the prompt literally says. Each child appears
    directly below its window, indented 2 spaces per level.
  - Prices: Glass 30, Frame 50. A window has no price of its own; its price() is
    just the sum of its frame and its four panes.

  Structure:
  - spec.md: the requirements as numbered points (S-1 to S-16) plus the test
    cases (T-1 to T-5).
  - src/window/:
    - item.go holds the Item interface.
    - leaves.go holds Glass ("Glass Panel") and Frame.
    - window.go holds Window, the node that contains the other parts.
  - src/main.go builds the three example trees and prints Display() and Price()
    for each.
  - test/window_test.go covers prices, the exact display output, the error cases
    when building a window, and that a single part and a whole window are used
    the same way.

  What else I decided:
  - Building a window: NewWindow(frame, [4]Item). The array type guarantees
    exactly four panes. A missing frame, a missing pane or a frame used as a
    pane returns ErrInvalidWindow.
  - Display: Window.Display() indents its children's output line by line. So the
    interface stays exactly as in the diagram, Price() and Display() with no
    depth parameter.
  - Thread safety: a window can't be changed after it's built, so reading it
    from several threads needs no locking. There is no separate store.

  The three examples:

  ┌────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────┐
  │  Example   │                       Layout                       │ Price │
  ├────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────┤
  │ Simple     │ four glass panes                                   │ 170   │
  │ window     │                                                    │       │
  ├────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────┤
  │ Split pane │ the example from the prompt: panes 0–2 glass, pane │ 310   │
  │            │  3 a sub-window                                    │       │
  ├────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────┤
  │ Muntin     │ three levels deep: pane 0 is a simple window, pane │ 590   │
  │ window     │  2 is the split-pane example                       │       │
  └────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────┘

